A||||5||kevin||Stereo Fit: Yes or No?||||1||
Z||000000||kevin||06-25-2000||08:09 PM||||I have access to a 1994 Bonneville cassette deck with built in equalizer.[$100] Will it fit in my 85? What obstacles are there, if any? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||06-25-2000||08:45 PM||||Stop by best buy, goto the installation counter (here it's actually outside the store). They have a pakage that covers almost all posibilities for $15 and all the wiring/antena adaptors for a couple more dollars.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||06-25-2000||09:28 PM||||If the one out of the B'ville is a DIN and a half size (oversize) then it should fit without any adapters.  I don't know about the harness, though.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||06-25-2000||10:26 PM||||Monkey's right, it should physically fit. At least staying with Pontiac means that most of the wires will be color coded the same. The newer GM stereos had a different harness connector and used a couple more wires, like a wire to the power antenna which of course most Fieros don't have, etc... Try to get them to include the harness connector and as much of the harness as possible.||||reg||1
Z||000004||84 Indy||06-25-2000||10:28 PM||||About 1990 GM changed their radio connectors<BR>Look for a theft repair kit for the 1994 bonneville probably at a Pontiac dealer. use this with a radio install harness for fiero. put the two together and you have a adapter harness. no need to cut the cars wires. you will also need a antenna adapter check any stereo store. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick's Dad||06-25-2000||10:37 PM||||Also, make sure that it was removed properly out of the car. The stereos often have a theft deterrent system that kicks in when the power has been cut. If this is the case, you'll have to go to the dealership and get a three digit code, a phone number and another code from the radio itself (When it is powered up). The service manual will provide all of this. <P>Power the radio, get the three digit code, then call the 800 number. It is autometed. You put in the number the radio gives you, and the system goves you an alternate code. Then, you punch in your new six digit code to the radio, and it will once again work.<P>I kind of remember this being the procedure, but it's been five years since I had to use it.||||reg||1
